- On Intentionalism: propose rather than receive (source: source.txt)
    Summary: Life structurally favors proposers over receivers, so practice intending before choosing. (¶1, ¶2, ¶3, ¶66)
        - 1 Gale-Shapley 1962 stable-matching work revealed proposer-receiver asymmetry. (¶4, ¶5, ¶6) [reported]
            - 1.1 Evidence: Deferred acceptance: one side proposes in order, other tentatively accepts or rejects. (¶7) [reported]
            - 1.2 Evidence: Proposers get best stable partner; receivers get worst; extremes are provable. (¶8) [reported]
            - 1.3 Nuance: Better outcomes come from proposing versus receiving, not talent or luck. (¶2, ¶3)
        - 2 Most real situations share proposer-receiver structure without running exact algorithm. (¶9, ¶10, ¶11)
            - 2.1 Example: Receiver quiz: Netflix scroll, displayed phones, posted jobs, proximity friendships. (¶12, ¶13, ¶14, ¶15, ¶16)
            - 2.2 Most of us mostly receive, choosing from presented rather than pursuing wanted. (¶17)
        - 3 Receiving yields acceptable outcomes that quietly fall short of best. (¶18, ¶19, ¶20)
            - 3.1 Informal proposer-receiver structures systematically favor whoever proposes. (¶21)
            - 3.2 Nuance: Comfortable outcomes block revolt; system stays stable by never screwing badly. (¶22)
            - 3.3 Nuance: Without practicing specific wanting, people cannot know what they miss. (¶23)
        - 4 Proposing needs intention, but intention develops through proposing. (¶24, ¶25)
            - 4.1 Nuance: Presented choices reveal least-disliked option, not what anyone would pursue freely. (¶26)
            - 4.2 The trap: intention is prerequisite and product of proposing. (¶27)
            - 4.3 Example: Algorithm-fed songs and swiped partners may be reactions mistaken for preferences. (¶28, ¶29, ¶30)
        - 5 Intention muscle builds progressively from small exercises upward. (¶31, ¶32)
            - 5.1 Example: Tonight decide the feeling wanted, then seek it without scrolling recommendations. (¶33, ¶34)
            - 5.2 Repeated practice surfaces patterns that become preferences then values. (¶35)
            - 5.3 Values come from practice, not from thought alone. (¶36)
        - 6 Major decisions mostly evaluate presented options, mistaking receipt for agency. (¶37, ¶38, ¶39, ¶40)
            - 6.1 Example: Intentional seekers of work, partners, cities gain systematically better outcomes. (¶41)
            - 6.2 Nuance: Advantage comes from structural side, not superior smarts or luck. (¶42)
        - 7 Receiver majorities let systems optimize for proposers instead of users. (¶43, ¶44)
            - 7.1 Example: Products, dating apps, feeds maximize engagement over genuine need or compatibility. (¶45)
            - 7.2 Platforms take optimal outcomes while users get participation-sustaining good-enough. (¶46)
            - 7.3 Nuance: Leaving feels harder than accepting; stability means resistance to change. (¶47)
        - 8 Understanding disadvantage changes nothing without switching to proposing. (¶48, ¶49, ¶50)
            - 8.1 Evidence: Proposing means deciding first, pursuing specifics, creating chances, accepting friction. (¶51)
            - 8.2 Ask what is wanted before every decision, then act despite inconvenience. (¶52)
            - 8.3 Nuance: Most refuse the effort; comfort plus atrophied wanting keeps receivers receiving. (¶53)
            - 8.4 Shifters gain structurally better outcomes even outside exact mathematics. (¶54)
            - 8.5 Nuance: Dynamic existence is settled; only willingness to act remains open. (¶55, ¶56)
        - 9 Start tomorrow morning with the first hour, then scale practice upward. (¶57, ¶58)
            - 9.1 Example: Name calm, energy, connection, or solitude; pursue it instead of notifications. (¶59, ¶60)
            - 9.2 Nuance: Notice scrolling versus intending; log satisfaction across small choices for a week. (¶61, ¶62)
            - 9.3 Scale to jobs, dating, purchases by deciding criteria before browsing options. (¶63)
            - 9.4 Muscle enables intentions, intentions become values, values make proposers. (¶64)
            - 9.5 Proposers end better across math and mess, not from specialness but position. (¶65, ¶66)
        - 10 Appendix gives progressive exercises for entertainment, shopping, jobs, relationships, days. (¶67, ¶68, ¶69)
            - 10.1 Example: Entertainment: name intention week one, seek week two, protect with timers week three. (¶70, ¶71, ¶72, ¶73)
            - 10.2 Example: Shopping: define problem, list must-haves, research features, ignore layout, 24-hour rule. (¶74, ¶75)
            - 10.3 Example: Toothpaste drill: identify need, find ingredient, buy it, notice packaging-free choosing. (¶76)
            - 10.4 Example: Career: set five non-negotiables, approach firms directly, apply only to strong matches. (¶77, ¶78, ¶79)
            - 10.5 Example: Dating: write relationship vision, require value alignment, filter first dates intentionally. (¶80, ¶81)
            - 10.6 Example: Friendship: name needed qualities, join matching communities, initiate specific activities. (¶82)
            - 10.7 Example: Daily: three morning intentions, evening proposer review, ten-second pause before choices. (¶83, ¶84, ¶85, ¶86)
            - 10.8 Example: Journal situation, mode, outcome; thirty days reveal proposer satisfaction pattern. (¶87, ¶88, ¶89)
            - 10.9 Intention means asking what is wanted before others answer; small reps grow capacity. (¶90, ¶91)
